# preferences.zd — the browser's own store, and the browser's own display.
#
# Two things a front end reaches for constantly and neither of which the
# language could say before:
#
#   1. A value that survives a reload but belongs to **this browser** —
#      `localStorage`. `client` state is gone on reload and `durable` state
#      is shared by every visitor, so neither of them is a preference.
#      `remembered` is: one value per browser profile per origin, shared
#      by that browser's tabs and by nobody else.
#
#   2. Whether the visitor's display asks for a dark scheme, or for less
#      animation. `media "…"` is a **signal**, not a read: the answer
#      changes when the visitor changes their system settings, and a view
#      that shows it changes with it. That is the whole reason it is a
#      construct rather than a `foreign` — reading `matchMedia(q).matches`
#      once at mount is a bug that is very easy to write and very hard to
#      notice, and this makes it unwritable.
#
# What `remembered` costs, stated where a reader will meet it:
#
#   * It may not be `secret` (E0313). Every script on the origin can read
#     the store and the entry outlives the visit, so a token in one is a
#     token published.
#   * It may not be `trusted` (E-INT-01), and reading one is always
#     Untrusted. `starting 0` below is the value on a browser that has
#     never opened this page; on every later visit the value is whatever
#     is in the store, which a previous session, another tab, or another
#     script on the origin may have put there. `visits + 1` is fine —
#     nothing here indexes a `trusted` collection with it — but the
#     compiler will not let it become the key to one.
#   * It may not be derived (E0321). A `remembered` signal is one the
#     browser kept, so it is `starting`, never `from`.

state visits is remembered Whole starting 0
state note   is remembered Text  starting ""

# The display, as it is right now. Both are ordinary `client` signals: the
# browser answers, so the read happens in the browser and crosses nothing.
state dark is client Truth from media "(prefers-color-scheme: dark)"
state calm is client Truth from media "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)"

view title is "Preferences"
    Column
        Heading "Preferences"

        Row
            Text "visits"
            Text visits

        Button "count this visit"
            on click
                add 1 to visits

        Button "forget"
            on click
                set visits to 0

        Text "note"
        Input note, hint is "type something and reload the page"
        Text note

        if dark
            Text "scheme: dark"
        otherwise
            Text "scheme: light"

        if calm
            Text "motion: reduced"
        otherwise
            Text "motion: full"
